xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2824:Dim-2 keeps proving the statements that are true (GMC(2), Shaska for every weight sign, no graded plane CE). Every known \(n\ge 3\) mechanism dies in the plane (Picard/\(d=3\) needle, no tangent-sweep, no graded escape). Eighty-five years without an example is evidence. Residue-A is the best formal candidate I have seen in this literature, but “passes the printed list” is exactly Theorem O, and an étale non-proper degree-6 endomorphism of \(\mathbb{A}^2\) is the thing JC2 says does not exist — consistency at vertices is cheap compared with algebraizing 126 series.
xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2961:ZHEGLOV-SCOPE.md:38:- **R4 → (c), partial (a).** Correspondence theorems are conceptual; but cutting/glueing the tail is algorithmic on truncated series — can numerically instantiate: take explicit commuting rank-1 pairs (e.g. KdV/Burchnall–Chaundy pairs), glue tail, check [Q,P]=1 to finite order, and vice versa. Distinguished-coefficient=0 claim testable on explicit rank-1 examples.
xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2965:ZHEGLOV-SCOPE.md:56:- **T3. Machinery on non-vacuous objects**: automorphism pairs (φ(x),φ(∂)) in A_1 and Burchnall–Chaundy rank-1 commuting pairs; compute normal forms to finite order; test L:Q_p tail shape, termination + non-maximal distance (intro's claim), S5 cut/glue roundtrip, distinguished-coefficient=0 (R:distinguished_coefficient). ~10-20 agent-h. Tests R4 + S2 end-to-end where hypotheses are satisfiable.
