xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:52:For a proposed horizontal divisor \(D\), Chapter 6 sets \(a=v_D(P)\), \(b=v_D(Q)\) and \(F_0=u_0T^a\), \(G_0=v_0T^b\). Lemmas 7.1–7.5 purport to build a flat \(k[s]\)-family whose fibers are the original image algebra and \(\kappa(D)[F_0,G_0]\). Lemma 7.6 is the dimension-semicontinuity link; Lemma 7.7 supplies generic dimension from dicriticality; Corollary 7.8 supplies special dimension at least one. Since the latter algebra lies in a one-variable polynomial ring over \(\kappa(D)\), Chapter 8 takes its dimension to be one.
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:92:| Cor. 7.8 | **FALSE** | If \(a=b=0\), then \(F_0,G_0\in\kappa(D)\) and \(\kappa(D)[F_0,G_0]=\kappa(D)\) has dimension zero, even when \(k(F_0,G_0)\) has transcendence degree one over \(k\). This is precisely the lost dicritical parameter. |
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:129:Purity does not repair this. Purity says that non-étaleness of the finite normal cover \(Y_0\to\mathbb A^2\) appears in codimension one; it does not say that the corresponding strict transform on \(X_0\) enters \(U\). In fact, because \(F\) is étale on \(U\), a ramification divisor of \(\sigma\) must have its strict transform in the boundary. A valid U-HIT theorem would therefore already eliminate the branch divisor and nearly complete the proof. Establishing it requires global control of the nonproperness curve, not generic fiber counting. None of the promoted purity/branch-locus results supplies that unconditional control.
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:131:## 6. Rees degeneration of affine dicriticals
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:156:When \(a+b>0\)—the very case needed later—the special member has zero Jacobian form. If absolute differentials include \(ds\), extra terms appear instead; neither version is a Keller family. The manuscript never constructs a family of affine-plane maps, never proves preservation of a dicritical component, and never obtains a nonzero relation over \(k\). Repair would require a different, strict degeneration together with a theorem retaining both the residue-curve parameter and the Keller identity. That is new core mathematics, not a local edit.
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:195:The charged integration states only that, under H2 (irreducible \(A_F\)), an affine-image dicritical with \(\mu=1\) is excluded; it explicitly leaves reducible \(A_F\) configurations at \(N=5\) and above (integration lines 22–35 and 65–73). Ramified dicriticals with \(\mu\ge2\) remain compatible with the promoted local identities and weighted budgets. Schenk claims much more: U-HIT would remove every horizontal source-boundary curve, without H2, degree restrictions, branch-shape hypotheses, or budget analysis.
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:197:That disparity pinpoints—not prejudges—the necessary new work. The new work would have to control the finite-open complement along the actual nonproperness curve, retain the normalization-selected branch valuation through degeneration, and show why every ramified/reducible survivor is impossible. Instead:
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:200:- the Rees argument conflates the physical divisor parameter \(\kappa(D)\), its place, and the grading parameter \(T\), then makes \(\kappa(D)\) the coefficient field and loses dicritical variation;
xmodel/schenk-audit-b-sol56-20260901.md:212:A repair would require, at minimum, a new theorem forcing a horizontal boundary divisor to meet \(U\) or otherwise excluding the nonproperness curve; alternatively it would require a genuine strict degeneration that preserves the relevant Keller/dicritical data and yields a relation over \(k\), plus complete canonical-divisor bookkeeping at infinity. None is present, and none follows from the charged promoted machinery. These are the heart of JC2, not editorial gaps. The standard finite-étale and ZMT tail remains conditional and does not change the verdict.
